<file path=".agents/skills/nativa-conformance/references/conformance.md">
# Conformance

## Principle

We do not rewrite what already exists. We reuse official suites and measure what
actually passes. A `SKIP` is not compatibility; it is declared debt.

## Suites

| Suite | What it measures | Where it lives |
|---|---|---|
| [Test262](https://github.com/tc39/test262) | ECMAScript language and built-ins | `.conformance/suites/test262` |
| [html5lib-tests](https://github.com/html5lib/html5lib-tests) | HTML tokenizer and tree construction | `.conformance/suites/html5lib-tests` |
| [WPT](https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt) | DOM, events, selectors, parsing | `.conformance/suites/wpt` |
| WPT `url/resources` | URL parsing | `.conformance/suites/wpt/url` |

Checkouts are pinned in `tools/conformance/bin/fetch_suites.dart`. They are not
versioned (`.conformance/` is in `.gitignore`).

## Statuses

| Status | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `PASS` | The test ran and passed. |
| `FAIL` | The test ran and failed, or the functionality does not exist. |
| `SKIP` | The runner cannot execute it or the feature is explicitly out of scope, with a reason. |
| `ERROR` | Runner failure (I/O, harness, crash). |

## Current baseline

These are the live numbers as of the last compatibility checkpoint. Use
`progress/status.md` and `progress/reports/` for the authoritative values.

| Suite | PASS | FAIL | SKIP | Rate |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Test262 (selected directories) | 19,577 | 413 | 1,108 | **97.93 %** |
| html5lib tokenizer | 5,759 | 43 | 1,025 | **99.26 %** |
| WPT URL | 636 | 260 | 0 | **70.98 %** |
| WPT `dom/nodes` | 168 | 115 | 44 | **59.36 %** |
| WPT `css/selectors` | 19 | 164 | 345 | **10.38 %** |
| WPT tree construction | 913 | 961 | 99 | **48.72 %** |

The rate excludes `SKIP`. A `SKIP` never counts as compatibility.

## Commands

```bash
cd tools/conformance

dart run bin/fetch_suites.dart --suite test262 --suite html5lib
dart run bin/fetch_suites.dart --suite wpt

dart run bin/run_test262.dart --limit 500
dart run bin/run_html5lib.dart
dart run bin/run_url.dart
dart run bin/run_wpt.dart --limit 200
dart run bin/run_html_tree.dart
```

Raw results go to `.conformance/results/*.jsonl` and `*.summary.json`. Dated
Markdown archives also stay in `.conformance/results/` and are ignored by git.
Only `progress/reports/<suite>-latest.md` is versioned.

## Known runner limitations

- **Test262 modules**: the bridge evaluates global scripts; tests with
  `flags: [module]` are reported as `SKIP`.
- **Test262 host APIs**: `$262.createRealm`, `$262.detachArrayBuffer`,
  `$262.agent`, and `$262.IsHTMLDDA` are `SKIP` (runner limitation, not a
  language limitation).
- **html5lib initial states**: the tokenizer starts in the Data state; fixtures
  with other `initialStates` are skipped.
- **html5lib DOCTYPE**: `DoctypeToken` exposes only the name; cases with public
  or system identifiers are skipped until the token is extended.
- **WPT**: a "lite" adapter. It does not support reftests, testdriver, workers,
  multiple globals, or the complete `testharness.js`. Those cases are skipped
  with a reason. Tests that use missing platform APIs (`fetch`,
  `getComputedStyle`, Shadow DOM, CSSOM, real layout) fail: those are real gaps.
  URL is implemented and measured separately.

## Policy for adding compatibility

1. Add the conformance test or a minimal regression test.
2. Implement the behavior.
3. Run the suite and compare with the baseline.
4. Update `progress/reports/` and the table in `progress/conformance.md`.
5. If something is skipped, record the reason and the corresponding issue/ADR.
</file>

<file path=".agents/skills/nativa-conformance/references/test-infrastructure.md">
# Test infrastructure and evidence

## Principle

Every run must be reproducible, comparable, and auditable. A result without a
command, version, seed, platform, and configuration is not evidence.

## Structure

```text
tools/
  conformance/   # official suites (Test262, WPT, html5lib, URL)
  benchmark/     # JS, Dart, bridge, DOM, CSS, style
  leaks/         # memory, retention, lifecycle
  fuzzing/       # (pending) generators, oracles, minimizer
  security/       # abuse, isolation, limits
  visual/        # (pending) golden images and tolerances
  stress/        # (pending) long-running and exhaustion
  compatibility/ # (pending) platform/version matrix
  test_all.sh    # current local runner
```

`progress/reports/` stores only the latest readable report per suite. Raw
results and dated Markdown archives go to ignored directories (`.conformance/`,
`benchmarks/results/`, `benchmarks/leaks/`, `benchmarks/security/`).

## Execution profiles

| Profile | When | Contents |
|---|---|---|
| `ci-fast` | Every change | unit + widget + analyze + scoped conformance + benchmark smoke |
| `ci-full` | Merge/nightly | all suites, regression thresholds |
| `nightly` | Daily | fuzzing, short long-running tests, visual, extended memory |
| `stress` | Weekly/dedicated | 24–72 h, controlled exhaustion, multi-platform |
| `release` | Before publishing | full matrix, reproducibility, installation, migrations |

## Minimum record per run

- commit and branch,
- NativaX and QuickJS-ng versions,
- OS, architecture, mode (JIT/AOT),
- configuration and profile,
- seed (if randomness is involved),
- date and duration,
- status per case (`PASS/FAIL/SKIP/ERROR/INCONCLUSIVE`),
- raw JSONL results,
- JSON and Markdown summaries,
- comparison with baseline and threshold/noise.

## Central orchestrator

`tools/test_all.sh` is the current local entry point. It must evolve into an
orchestrator that:

1. Discovers suites and profiles.
2. Runs in order and saves partial results if a suite fails.
3. Applies timeouts and limits.
4. Generates one report linking the per-suite reports.
5. Returns distinct exit codes for test failure, regression, and infrastructure
   error.
6. Allows reproducing a case with `--only` and a seed.

## Fuzzing

- Deterministic generators with a seed.
- Oracles: DOM parser/serialization, CSSOM, selectors, JS engine.
- Differential testing only against references that are actually available and
  documented.
- Automatic case minimization.
- Versioned corpus of failures and seeds.
- A fuzzing failure becomes a regression test before it is closed.
- Time/memory limits; dangerous cases run in an isolated process.

## Security

- Host, file, process, and unexposed-API access tests.
- CPU, memory, stack, node, and storage limits.
- Malformed inputs and hostile resources.
- Reentrancy and operations after `dispose()`.
- Dependencies: vulnerabilities, licenses, and maintenance.
- Isolation between contexts/documents.
- Destructive scenarios do not run by default.

## Visual regression

- Golden images per platform/DPI/font.
- Explicit tolerances (antialiasing, hinting).
- Cases: flex, positioning, stacking, images, text, scroll, resize.
- Do not approve a visual difference just because a functional test passes.
- Do not fail because of font differences without comparing on the same
  platform.

## Accessibility and input

- Semantics tree (roles, labels, headers, links, buttons).
- Focus and tab order.
- Keyboard: keydown/keyup, activation, escape, arrows.
- Text scaling and contrast.
- Accessible forms.
- Flutter tests with `SemanticsTester`/`tester.ensureSemantics`.
- Do not claim full accessibility just because labels exist.

## Long-running tests

- Run a reference app with periodic activity.
- Measure RSS, QuickJS heap, nodes, listeners, timers, and handles.
- Check latency (p50/p95/p99) at the start, middle, and end.
- Detect sustained growth, not just that the process is alive.
- Store periodic samples and compare trends.
- Limit RAM/time and abort with a partial report.

## Regressions

- Benchmarks: per-category threshold plus measured noise (MAD/median).
- Conformance: compare PASS/FAIL per test, not just the percentage.
- Leaks: compare state and trend, not a single sample.
- Visual: tolerance per platform.
- Every confirmed regression needs an issue, a minimal case, and a test.

## Reproducing failures

A failure must include:

1. commit,
2. command,
3. profile/configuration,
4. seed,
5. minimal input or steps,
6. expected and actual result,
7. logs/JSONL.

Without these it is an anecdote, not a reproducible bug.

## Quality gating

- `ci-fast` must be fast and stable; if flaky, fix it or quarantine it with an
  owner.
- `ci-full` may be slower; "green" by omitting suites is not allowed.
- Benchmarks do not block because of noise; only a confirmed regression blocks.
- `INCONCLUSIVE` leaks do not block, but they are recorded and reviewed.
- An infrastructure `ERROR` blocks publication.
- Do not publish a platform without a real build and tests on that platform.

## Current state

- Conformance, benchmarks, and leaks have tools and formats.
- Fuzzing, security coverage, visual, accessibility, stress, and compatibility
  are pending; they are not presented as implemented.
- The central orchestrator is still `tools/test_all.sh`; profile evolution is
  part of phase 6.
</file>

<file path=".agents/skills/nativa-security/references/security.md">
# Security

## Threat model

NativaX executes HTML, CSS, and JavaScript that may be hostile. The runtime
trusts none of them.

| Asset | Risk | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|
| Process / UI | Infinite script or microtask loop | 1 s budget per entry, 10k jobs per drain |
| Memory | Unbounded heap | 64 MB per runtime (configurable), explicit GC |
| Stack | Deep recursion | 256 KB by default; TCO is not supported |
| DOM | Huge tree | 100k nodes, depth 256 |
| HTML parser | Malformed input / infinite nodes | Node limits and error recovery |
| Storage | Unlimited quota | 5 MB of characters |
| Host | Internal details leaked to JS | Generic error responses |
| Events | Orphaned listeners | `RemovalAwareListener`, cleanup on unmount |

## JS ↔ Dart boundary

- Scripts see only numeric ids; never Dart objects.
- Arguments and results are validated and decoded from JSON.
- An unexpected host error is reported as `internal host error`, without a Dart
  stack or message.
- `app.invoke` is a sensitive surface: the app decides which handlers to
  register and must treat arguments as untrusted.

## What is not implemented (and therefore cannot be assumed)

- No OS-level sandbox: the engine runs in the app process.
- No isolation between documents: one runtime = one document.
- No CSP or origin policy.
- No guarantee that destructive workloads are safe without the configured
  limits. Timeouts on the same thread do not always interrupt a JavaScript
  infinite loop.
- External resources are policy-gated and denied by default; this is not a
  network sandbox.

## Current audit

The executable suite lives in `tools/security`. The live Linux status is in
`progress/status.md`; the current audit reports 14 PASS, 0 FAIL, and 1 SKIP.
See `../../../docs/security/audit.md`, `../../../docs/security/threat-model.md`,
`../../../docs/security/test-plan.md`, and
`../../../docs/security/platform-matrix.md`.

## Contribution rules

1. Every new API exposed to JS needs type validation and limits.
2. Do not add APIs that can read host paths, environment variables, or memory.
3. `JavaScriptHostError` messages must be stable and contain no sensitive data.
4. Every new limit needs a security/abuse test.
</file>

<file path=".agents/skills/nativa-verification/references/coding-standards.md">
# Coding standards

## Dart

- SDK `^3.10.0`, strict null safety.
- Prefer `final`, `const`, patterns, and exhaustive `switch`.
- Public APIs use `///`; internal details live in `lib/src/`.
- Domain errors use dedicated types (`DomException`, `JavaScriptException`,
  `HtmlLimitException`). Do not throw generic `Exception('...')` from public
  APIs.
- Avoid `dynamic` except at JSON/FFI boundaries, and convert it immediately.
- Limits (nodes, depth, memory, time) are part of the contract, not hidden
  options.

## FFI and C

- Only scalars, pointers, and UTF-8 buffers cross the boundary.
- Never expose `JSValue` to Dart.
- Every buffer returned by C is released with `nx_free`, including on error
  paths.
- The host callback must not throw through C: catch and convert failures into a
  JSON error.
- Use `-O3`, `-DNDEBUG`, and hidden visibility by default; bridge symbols are
  exported explicitly with `NX_EXPORT`.

## Flutter

- Use custom `RenderObject`s with `parentUsesSize: true` when measuring a child.
- `computeDryLayout` must be equivalent to `performLayout` without side effects.
- Recreate and dispose `TextSpan`s with `TapGestureRecognizer` on rebuild.
- Release `ValueNotifier`s and listeners in `dispose`.
- Keep JavaScript logic out of widgets.

## Tests

- Test names are in English and are complete sentences.
- One test = one behavior; use `group` to organize related tests.
- Conformance tests do not mix with unit tests.
- Failures must be reproducible with a documented command.

## Documentation

- `docs/status.md` is the session summary; `progress/` is the source of truth.
- ADRs belong in `docs/adr/` (pending) and are linked from `.agents/`.
- Every new limitation is documented the same day.
</file>

<file path=".agents/skills/nativa-verification/references/working-method.md">
# Demanding work method

This is the mandatory protocol for every change in Nativa. Do not skip a step
"because it is obvious".

## 1. Inspect before writing

- Read `AGENTS.md`, `docs/status.md`, `progress/`, and the relevant `.agents/` docs.
- Locate the package, API, and existing tests.
- Check existing limits, contracts, and decisions.
- Identify what cannot yet be measured or implemented.
- Do not invent APIs, duplicate subsystems, or replace the engine.

## 2. Define the acceptance criterion

Before coding, write down in the response or in `progress/`:

- what behavior changes,
- which test proves it,
- which command verifies it,
- which metric measures it (if applicable),
- what is explicitly out of scope.

Do not start without a verifiable criterion.

## 3. Design the smallest cut

- Prefer small, reversible changes.
- Keep parser, DOM, CSS, renderer, runtime, and tooling separate.
- Reuse the conventions in `tools/conformance`, `tools/benchmark`, and
  `tools/leaks`.
- Do not mix large refactors with a feature.
- Document approximations and limits in the same change.

## 4. Implement with tests

1. Write the failing test (or the fixture that reproduces the bug).
2. Implement the minimum that makes it pass.
3. Add edge cases: null/undefined, limits, errors, lifecycle.
4. Run:

```bash
dart test            # pure Dart package, from its directory
flutter test         # Flutter package, from its directory
dart analyze
flutter analyze
```

5. Do not continue with unexplained red tests.

## 5. Verify for real

- Run the new test and the existing tests.
- Run `./tools/test_all.sh` with the game closed.
- Run the affected conformance suite if parser/CSS/JS changed.
- Run benchmarks/leaks if performance or lifecycle changed.
- Check that no compatibility rate regressed without documentation.

## 6. Measure without fooling yourself

Performance:

- warm-up,
- multiple rounds,
- median and dispersion (MAD),
- percentiles,
- checksum or identical work,
- environment metadata,
- baseline comparison with both a threshold **and** measured noise.

Memory:

- baseline,
- batches,
- explicit release and GC,
- positive control (retention) and negative control (release),
- resource counters,
- trend, not a single sample,
- `FAIL` only with evidence; otherwise `INCONCLUSIVE`.

## 7. Document in the same change

- `progress/status.md`: state and numbers.
- `progress/log.md`: what was done and the commands used.
- `progress/conformance.md`: rates and gaps.
- `docs/`: API contracts, limits, and guides.
- `.agents/`: new decisions or requirements.
- `docs/status.md`: session summary.

## 8. Report

Every delivery includes:

1. What was implemented.
2. Files created or modified.
3. Exact commands.
4. Real results (tests, rates, medians, memory).
5. What could not be measured and why.
6. A concrete next step.

Do not use "it should work", "probably", or "I think it passes" as evidence.

## 9. Integrity rules

- Do not use `SKIP` to make numbers look better.
- Do not delete slow tests.
- Do not change JavaScript semantics to win a benchmark.
- Do not simulate V8/JavaScriptCore.
- Do not fill missing results with zeros.
- Do not claim 70 % if it was not measured.
- Do not close a phase with pending criteria.
- Do not claim a leak from a single RSS sample.
- Do not hide a regression because the average improved.

## 10. Closing checklist

- [ ] Acceptance criterion written.
- [ ] New and existing tests pass.
- [ ] Analyze is clean.
- [ ] Affected conformance measured (if applicable).
- [ ] Benchmarks/leaks measured (if applicable).
- [ ] Documentation and progress updated.
- [ ] Limitations stated.
- [ ] No invented numbers.
- [ ] Clear next step.

## 11. Phase state

- **Phase 4 is open** until the target WPT suites are ≥ 70 %.
- The benchmark and leak suites are measurement tools, not substitutes for
  conformance.
- A phase is marked ✅ only when its verifiable criterion is met.

## 12. Compatibility increments

- Keep compatibility commits separate from structural refactors.
- Stop, report, and checkpoint after each real ~1 % compatibility gain.
- A measurement-adapter fix is not a real compatibility gain.
- Never mix a compatibility increment with game changes.

## 13. Session report format

```text
Objective:
Changes:
Evidence (commands + results):
Conformance:
Performance/memory:
Limitations:
Next step:
```
</file>

